    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who are you?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'you' directly with 'who', combining 'you' with 'who' and 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что ты улыбаешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What are you smiling at?' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'are smiling'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Давай, отвечай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, answer.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'let's', followed by 'answer'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Айнура, это японец?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Aynura, is this a Japanese person?' Chef Barinov turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'Japanese man' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'no' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он не наш.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is not ours.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'he' directly with 'not', combining 'he' with 'not' and 'our'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Слушай, ты перепутал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, you made a mistake.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'listen', followed by 'mixed up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Суши делают рядом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They make sushi next door.' Chef Barinov expresses this naturally by combining the word for 'sushi' with 'they do' and 'nearby'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Шеф, это новый повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, this is the new cook.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'new' directly with 'chef', combining 'new' with 'chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Какой повар?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What cook?' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'chef'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Он будет делать суши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He will make sushi.' Senya chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'will be' for the ongoing action, followed by 'do' and 'sushi'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Иди отсюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Get out of here.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'go', followed by 'from here'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
